      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: stop doctor misreporting trusted-proxy gateways as unauthenticated",
        "body": "Closes #91284\n\nAI-assisted: yes (Codex). I reviewed and understand the change.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nAn exposed Gateway using complete trusted-proxy authentication was reported by `openclaw doctor` as “without authentication.” Doctor then recommended generating a token, even though token auth is mutually exclusive with trusted-proxy mode.\n\nThat false critical is confusing for reverse-proxy deployments and can steer operators away from the security boundary they intentionally configured.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nDoctor now recognizes trusted-proxy authentication only when the configuration can satisfy the Gateway’s existing startup and request-authorization contracts. The implementation reuses the runtime owners—resolved auth, startup validation, HTTP authorization, source checks, and browser-origin matching—instead of creating a second permissive auth policy inside doctor.\n\nThe downgrade remains deliberately fail-closed:\n\n- every trusted proxy source must be a canonical, usable, host-scoped address: exact IP, `/32`, or `/128`;\n- subnets, default routes, invalid/non-unicast entries, disallowed loopback, host-interface sources, and interface-inspection failures remain `CRITICAL`;\n- missing/invalid identity headers, unusable `allowUsers`, and actual shared-token conflicts remain `CRITICAL`;\n- wildcard origins, dangerous Host-header origin fallback, and missing/nonmatchable explicit browser origins remain independent `CRITICAL` findings;\n- independent proxy and browser-origin defects are reported together in one doctor run.\n\nAn inactive token SecretRef remains inactive, matching Gateway startup behavior; plaintext or environment-derived token conflicts still fail closed.\n\nThis is a doctor classification/remediation change only. It does not change runtime authentication, config/schema, protocol behavior, defaults, or stored data.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators with a complete host-scoped trusted-proxy deployment now receive an accurate exposure warning with proxy/firewall guidance instead of an unauthenticated critical and token-repair advice. Unsafe or incomplete deployments keep specific critical findings.\n\nScope metrics:\n\n- config/default surfaces added, changed, or removed: 0;\n- migrations or stored-data changes: 0;\n- runtime auth behavior changes: 0;\n- diagnostic classification/remediation changes: 1 bounded surface.\n\n## Evidence\n\nExact control base (canonical `main` at rebase): `87de81a5fd4a2d4e3ef8bff9af22a3ed2ab8b4e8`  \nExact patched head: `0a526e46e7064ed1ec168097139f6e041b1195c0`\n\nA synthetic before/after VHS recording shows:\n\n- control red: the exact base misreports the LAN-bound Gateway as unauthenticated and recommends token repair;\n- fixed: the exact head recognizes trusted-proxy authentication and retains network-exposure/firewall guidance;\n- focused proof: 237 tests pass across five files and two Vitest shards (141 Gateway + 96 doctor).\n\nThe 1400×900 recording uses only RFC 5737 test data, `x-forwarded-user`, and `https://control.example.test`. All 696 MP4 frames and 418 GIF frames decoded successfully and were timing/signal/privacy audited. No local paths, personal identifiers, credentials, private-network details, or tokens are visible.\n\n- MP4 SHA-256: `1f1843e4c4d19feab121c5027a2e00b910b275de2ad104ea0d7bb414ae65b3f2`\n- GIF SHA-256: `aaeef92cfb748b105008e957ef949da038cccd8bdd5c026ffe75d55c0966dcc7`\n\nExact-head verification:\n\n- focused runtime/doctor tests: 237/237 passed;\n- changed-surface gate: passed formatting, lint, core/core-test typechecks, dependency/API/plugin-boundary guards, database-first/media/sidecar guards, webhook/pairing guards, and zero runtime import cycles;\n- commit-mode autoreview: clean, no actionable findings; verdict “patch is correct”;\n- source trust: maintained contributor branch, verified exact Git objects, clean worktree.\n\nBoundary: the recording proves doctor CLI classification/remediation and the focused runtime contracts. It does not claim a live remote reverse-proxy/browser login flow.\n\nOrigin-check findings are intentionally trusted-proxy-only: proxy auth is ambient (header-based), making the Origin allowlist the CSRF boundary; token/password modes require explicit per-request credentials. A doctor origin audit for those modes would be a separate follow-up.",
